(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement describes cochlear damage from long-term chronic noise exposure?","choices":["Above 120 decibels, causing tympanic membrane rupture","Above 60 decibels, causing endolymphatic hydrops","Above 85 decibels, by stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis","Above 85 decibels, causing reversible stereocilia fatigue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-term chronic exposure to sound above 85 decibels induces irreversible stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis of the acoustic hair cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which medication class is a cataract risk?","choices":["Chronic corticosteroids","Chronic beta-blockers","Chronic antihistamines","Chronic statins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronic corticosteroid use is the medication cause of cataract, alongside aging, diabetes, ocular trauma, Down syndrome and ultraviolet light. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What are the two connective tissue layers of the dermis?","choices":["Papillary, the thin superficial layer, and reticular, the dense deeper layer","Basale and spinosum","Corneum and lucidum","The papillary and the subcutaneous, being the superficial and the deepest layers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The dermis consists of two connective tissue layers: the thin superficial papillary layer and the dense deeper reticular layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which description of leukonychia is accurate?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed","Increased capillary density with release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the nail bed tissue itself","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually from harmless nail bed damage","A halt of keratin production, associated with illness and malnourishment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leukonychia, or Mee's lines, shows white streaks or spots from temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually from harmless nail bed damage, though it is also seen with heavy metal poisoning. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which structural epidermal changes occur in psoriasis?","choices":["Epidermal hyperplasia, loss of the stratum granulosum, and failed lipid secretion causing xeroderma","Thinning of the epidermis with slowed keratinocyte division","Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with fluid accumulating between the two layers of the skin itself","Loss of melanocytes from the basal layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active T cells drive keratinocyte proliferation into epidermal hyperplasia, the stratum granulosum is lost, and epidermal cells stop secreting lipids. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Where must light be focused for an image to form?","choices":["On the optic disc, where the nerve exits","On the lens itself, which then transmits the signal","On the retina, after refraction through the cornea and lens","On the choroid, after refraction through the cornea alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — image formation requires light refracted through the cornea and lens to be focused onto the retina, where rods and cones convert photons to signals. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does anticoagulation favor POSTERIOR rather than anterior epistaxis?","choices":["The posterior mucosa is thinner","The anterior septum has no blood supply","Anticoagulants are concentrated posteriorly","Posterior bleeds reflect systemic causes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anterior causes are local insults to the mucosa; posterior causes act on the vessels and on clotting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which ion movement depolarizes the cochlear hair cell?","choices":["Influx from the perilymph across the basilar membrane","Release from intracellular stores after glutamate binding","Efflux into the perilymph through voltage-gated channels","Influx from the endolymph through the tip-link channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Deflected stereocilia open the tip-link channels, permitting rapid influx from the endolymph into the hair cell. Depolarization then opens voltage-gated channels and glutamate is released onto cranial nerve VIII. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What distinguishes dry from wet gangrene?","choices":["Dry is acute; wet is chronic","Dry involves fibrinous exudate; wet involves serous exudate","Dry is coagulative necrosis; wet is liquefactive necrosis arising from infection","Dry gangrene occurs on mucosal surfaces; wet gangrene occurs on serosal surfaces"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gangrenous inflammation is a severe form of necrosis with or without superimposed bacterial infection, dry being coagulative and wet liquefactive from infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which feature defines granulocytes under light microscopy?","choices":["A kidney-shaped nucleus that permits the cell to transmigrate between two adjacent endothelial cells nearby","Azurophilic granules that function purely as antigen-presenting structures","Surface receptors that bind the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G","Differently staining cytoplasmic granules: membrane-bound digestive enzymes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The granules are membrane-bound enzymes whose primary action is digesting what has been phagocytized. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What do promoters do?","choices":["They cause the permanent deoxyribonucleic acid damage that starts the process","They repair the deoxyribonucleic acid damage caused by initiators","They cause tumor cells to detach and enter the circulation","They drive proliferation of cells already damaged by an initiator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sustained or enhanced proliferation raises the risk of successive mutations leading to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does the degree of differentiation of a neoplasm measure?","choices":["How rapidly the tumor mass is enlarging over time","How many of its cells are in the proliferative pool at any moment","How closely it resembles comparable normal cells in appearance and function","How far it has spread from the primary site within the patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — differentiation is the degree a neoplasm resembles comparable normal cells, which is the basis of histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does aplasia differ from agenesis?","choices":["The primordial tissue never forms, so the organ is completely absent","The organ develops only partially, leaving a functional deficiency","The organ enlarges because its individual cells enlarge","The primordial tissue exists but fails to develop into the mature organ"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in aplasia the primordial tissue exists but fails to develop into the mature organ, whereas in agenesis it never formed at all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What do the lids, cornea and conjunctiva have in common as the outermost ocular structures?","choices":["They contain the highest density of nerve endings","They are the last to be affected in systemic disease","They are more susceptible to infection, trauma and environmental change","They are the only structures visible without an ophthalmoscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — as the outer, easily visible structures, the lids, cornea and conjunctiva face the environment directly and are more susceptible to infection, trauma and environmental change. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A temporal bone fracture causes conductive loss by which mechanism?","choices":["Obstruction of the external canal","Discontinuity of the ossicular chain","Stiffness of the ossicular chain","Mass loading of the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The chain is physically disrupted so the wave cannot be conducted across it. Ossicular necrosis produces the same end result by erosion rather than trauma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the three linked vascular mechanisms?","choices":["Exudation, then vasodilation, then endothelial contraction","Vasodilation of arterioles, then endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability, then exudation","Endothelial contraction, then vasodilation of the arterioles, then leukocyte transmigration into the tissue","Endothelial contraction, then exudation, then vasodilation of arterioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation raises blood flow, endothelial contraction opens the capillaries, and fluid and blood proteins then move into the interstitial spaces. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What distinguishes atrophy from hypoplasia?","choices":["Atrophy is shrinkage of a tissue or organ that had already formed and matured normally","Atrophy is partial development of an organ that never reached mature size","Atrophy is enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge","Atrophy is disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the timing is what separates them: hypoplasia is a developmental failure. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What do regulatory (suppressor) T cells do?","choices":["Returning immunity to normal after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Producing antibody directed against antigens the host has encountered previously","Presenting phagocytosed material to naive T cells","Killing tumor cells directly through granule release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Regulatory (suppressor) T cells return the immune system to normal operation after infection and prevent autoimmunity. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which systemic diseases are strongly linked to scleritis?","choices":["Sarcoidosis and choroidal melanoma","Rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and systemic vasculitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis","Diabetes mellitus and hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — scleritis, an emergency of the deep sclera with boring pain and a risk of scleral melting, is strongly linked to rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and systemic vasculitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which factors cause bullae?","choices":["Androgen stimulation of sebaceous glands","Mechanical factors, infections, toxins, chemicals and autoimmunity","Increased melanin production and cumulative ultraviolet light exposure alone","Capillary rupture and thrombocytopenia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with bullous impetigo as the bacterial example and bullous pemphigoid as the autoimmune one. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which description fits the semicircular canals?","choices":["Linear acceleration and gravitational position, via the otoliths","Rotational acceleration, by endolymph bending the cupula","Sound frequency, via the basilar membrane","Middle ear pressure, via the Eustachian tube"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three loops in the sagittal, coronal and transverse planes. The fluid lags behind the head, the gelatinous cupula bends, and the hair cell stereocilia inside it are stimulated. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the cell population of the epidermis?","choices":["Keratinocytes (about 90 percent), melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells","Fibroblasts, macrophages, adipocytes and mast cells, with fibroblasts predominating","Melanocytes, Merkel cells, fibroblasts and histiocytes, with melanocytes predominating","Keratinocytes and melanocytes only, in roughly equal proportion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The epidermis holds keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells, and keratinocytes, which produce keratin, make up about 90 percent of it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the purposes of cancer staging?","choices":["To measure how closely the tumor resembles the normal tissue it came from","To identify which gene alterations caused the tumor","To determine whether the tumor is benign or malignant","Extent of spread, prognosis, and guiding management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three. Staging answers a different question from histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the fate of a monocyte once it leaves the bloodstream?","choices":["A dendritic cell that migrates to the thymus","A tissue macrophage that clears debris and attacks microorganisms","A plasma cell that secretes antibody directly into the interstitium","A fibroblast that lays down scar tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes eventually leave the bloodstream to become tissue macrophages with exactly these roles. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are the risk factors for rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Chronic corticosteroid use, Down syndrome and ultraviolet light exposure","Posterior vitreous detachment, age, severe myopia, trauma and lattice degeneration","Diabetes, prior vitrectomy and retinal trauma surgery","Sarcoidosis, malignant hypertension and melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — posterior vitreous detachment, age, severe myopia, trauma and lattice degeneration set up the full-thickness break through which liquefied vitreous enters. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma with a glandular growth pattern called?","choices":["An adenocarcinoma","An adenoma","A squamous cell carcinoma","A sarcoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumor, and one with a glandular growth pattern is an adenocarcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the lipid layer of the tear film do?","choices":["It provides the bulk volume of the tears","It anchors the tear film to the corneal epithelium","It carries immunoglobulin to the ocular surface","It prevents evaporation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — meibomian glands in the tarsal plates produce the lipid layer, which prevents evaporation; when they are obstructed, as in blepharitis, tears evaporate excessively. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which causes produce CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Presbycusis and noise trauma","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere disease and labyrinthitis","Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis and cerebellar tumor","Otosclerosis and cerumen impaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All involve the brainstem or cerebellum, which is why the nystagmus can be vertical and is not suppressed by fixation, and why other neurological signs appear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statement about the causes of otitis externa is accurate?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae; fungal disease follows swimming","Moraxella catarrhalis alone; fungal disease follows trauma","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus; fungal after antibiotics","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus; fungal disease follows steroid use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus cause 80 to 90 percent of cases; fungal disease from Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans often follows prolonged antibiotic use. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is melanoma risk inherited?","choices":["As an autosomal recessive trait with complete penetrance","As an X-linked trait","It is entirely environmental with no inherited component","As an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though ultraviolet light and oxidative stress remain the primary driving factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is a hypopyon?","choices":["Yellow debris beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Pus in the anterior chamber","Blood in the anterior chamber","Fluid beneath the neurosensory retina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a hypopyon is a collection of pus in the anterior chamber, the inflammatory layer seen in the front of a severely inflamed eye. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which description matches the appearance of a neutrophil?","choices":["Kidney-shaped nucleus with abundant cytoplasm that is usually agranulated when it is viewed on light microscopy","Multilobed nucleus, with fine pale lilac granules making the cytoplasm look transparent","Bi- or tri-lobed nucleus with a number of coarse granules","Bi-lobed nucleus with pink-orange granules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil's multilobed nucleus can look like several nuclei, and fine pale lilac granules make its cytoplasm look transparent. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pair of conditions are examples of autoimmunity causing chronic inflammation?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Atherosclerosis and silicosis","Asthma and hay fever","Trauma and bee sting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus are autoimmune diseases, and autoimmunity is one of the three causes of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which description of Terry's nails is accurate?","choices":["Increased capillary density together with vascular endothelial growth factor release into the tissue locally over time","Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, associated with aging, liver disease, congestive heart failure or diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Terry's nails come from overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, leaving the nail opaque except for a dark band at the tip. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which pair of tuning fork findings indicates conductive hearing loss?","choices":["Weber to the affected ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber is equal; Rinne shows bone greater than air bilaterally","Weber to the AFFECTED ear; Rinne shows bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the UNAFFECTED ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With sound wave transmission to the oval window defective, Weber lateralizes to the AFFECTED ear and Rinne is abnormal, with bone conduction greater than air. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains which combination of material?","choices":["Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris — that is, pus","Fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork over the serosal surface","Low-protein plasma with minimal cellular content","A superficial necrotic layer forming a membrane over affected mucosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris, which is pus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does panophthalmitis differ from endophthalmitis?","choices":["It involves only the vitreous","It is limited to the anterior segment and spares the vitreous","It spares the sclera","It involves every layer of the globe and may enter the orbit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pan- means all: panophthalmitis involves every layer of the globe and can spread into the orbit, beyond the intraocular infection of endophthalmitis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is sebum primarily composed of?","choices":["Collagen and elastin","Triglycerides, wax esters and squalene","Water, sodium and chloride electrolytes","Keratin and keratohyalin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, produced by the sebaceous glands associated with hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do the borders of benign and malignant tumors differ?","choices":["Both have well circumscribed borders, and they are distinguished only by growth rate","Both have ragged borders, and they are distinguished only by whether they metastasize","Benign is well circumscribed and compresses; malignant is ragged and invades","Benign has a ragged border and infiltrates surrounding tissue; malignant is well circumscribed and compresses it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the benign tumor pushes surrounding tissue aside, the malignant one infiltrates and goes through it. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which inherited tumor suppressor alteration is associated with malignant melanoma?","choices":["p16, also called INK4a","Retinoblastoma protein","Adenomatous polyposis coli","BRCA-1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — p16 (INK4a) is an inherited tumor suppressor alteration associated with malignant melanoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary from a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["A primary lesion is the direct result of the underlying disease and retains its original unmodified appearance; a secondary lesion has been modified over time","A primary lesion is palpable and a secondary lesion is flat","A primary lesion is benign and a secondary lesion is malignant","A primary lesion is the one that appears first in time, and a secondary lesion is any lesion that appears later at a different site elsewhere on the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble the lesion it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which description fits allergic rhinitis?","choices":["Immune complex deposition, type 3 hypersensitivity","T cell mediated, type 4 delayed hypersensitivity","IgE-mediated type 1 hypersensitivity, with CLEAR rhinorrhea","IgG-mediated type 2 hypersensitivity with purulent rhinorrhea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 1 hypersensitivity driven by IgE (immunoglobulin E) against inhaled allergens. The CLEAR discharge separates it from bacterial sinusitis, which is purulent. Allergic shiners complete the picture. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which combination of granule color and nuclear shape identifies the eosinophil?","choices":["No visible granules, kidney-shaped nucleus","Pale lilac granules, multilobed nucleus","Coarse dark granules, bi- or tri-lobed nucleus","Pink-orange granules, bi-lobed nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cytoplasm is full of granules that assume a characteristic pink-orange color, with a bi-lobed nucleus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which layer of the epidermis is the site of cellular division?","choices":["The stratum corneum, the outermost layer","The granular layer, known as the stratum granulosum","The stratum lucidum","The basal layer, or stratum basale, the deepest layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes, which also contains melanocytes and Merkel cells. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Vasodilation of the arterioles supplying an injured region produces which immediate effect?","choices":["Increased blood flow to the injured region","Reduced hydrostatic pressure within the capillary bed","Contraction of the capillary endothelium","Movement of blood proteins into the interstitium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles increases blood flow. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which four features define dysplasia?","choices":["Invasion through the basement membrane with spread to regional lymph nodes","Disordered epithelial growth, varied cell size and shape, lost architecture, darker larger nuclei","Orderly growth, typically mesenchymal; uniform cell size; preserved architecture; nuclei of normal size and staining","Complete replacement of one mature epithelium by another mature epithelium, with normal architecture retained"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — dysplasia is disordered epithelial growth with varied cell size and shape, lost architectural orientation and darker, larger nuclei; it may progress to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In the five main processes of the inflammatory response, what immediately follows the migration of neutrophils?","choices":["Chemotaxis","Increased permeability","Exudation of plasma proteins","Increased blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The order runs increased blood flow, increased permeability, migration of neutrophils, chemotaxis, then leukocyte recruitment and activation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which description of seborrheic dermatitis is accurate?","choices":["Autoimmune T cell activation, affecting the elbows and knees","Ultraviolet damage, affecting sun-exposed skin","Microbiome dysbiosis, altered immunity and barrier dysfunction, in the sebaceous-rich scalp, face and upper trunk","A single fungal pathogen acting alone, affecting mainly the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet rather than the sebaceous areas of the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cause is incompletely understood, but dysbiosis, altered immunity and barrier dysfunction contribute, in sebaceous-rich scalp, face and upper trunk. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which description of how dermatophytes infect the skin is accurate?","choices":["Spores secrete keratinases and proteases that digest keratin, typically in the stratum corneum","They invade epidermal basal cells through microabrasions and drive proliferation of the epidermis above them over time","They release histamine from mast cells producing wheals","They proliferate within the dermis, digesting collagen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophyte spores secrete keratinases and proteases that digest keratin in skin, hair and nails, and infection typically occurs in the stratum corneum. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which statement about bacterial tonsillitis and its abscess complication is accurate?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae; bilateral exudate","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; fever above 39 degrees","Haemophilus influenzae; odynophagia","Group A Streptococcus; asymmetry and trismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tonsillitis is usually Group A Streptococcus, and asymmetric tonsillar deviation with trismus indicates a complicating tonsillar abscess. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does eradicating Helicobacter pylori do to cancer risk?","choices":["It reverses established gastric adenocarcinoma","It reduces gastric cancer risk, and some early MALT lymphomas regress","It has no effect on cancer risk once colonization has occurred","It increases the risk, because the inflammatory response is protective"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — eradicating Helicobacter pylori with antibiotics can reduce gastric cancer risk and may induce regression of some early MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphomas. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What do Nx, N0 and N1 to N2 or higher each mean?","choices":["Nx, nodes not yet sampled; N0, one node involved; N1 upwards, two or more nodes","Nx, nodes removed surgically; N0, nodes present but normal in size; N1 upwards, enlarged nodes","Cannot be assessed; none; increasing number and range involved","Nx, no regional nodal metastasis; N0, nodes cannot be assessed; N1 upwards, distant metastasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — x always means cannot be assessed, 0 always means none found, and the numbers rise with the number and range of nodes involved. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does aqueous humor nourish?","choices":["The extraocular muscles","The avascular lens and cornea","The retina and the choroid","The optic nerve head"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — made by the ciliary body, aqueous flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber and nourishes the lens and cornea, both of which lack blood vessels. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which feature of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo separates it most cleanly from the other peripheral causes?","choices":["The vertigo lasts several hours","It follows a viral upper respiratory infection","There is NO hearing loss","It produces aural fullness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meniere disease and labyrinthitis both take hearing with them; this does not, because the otoconia are a vestibular problem that never touches the cochlea. Attacks also last under a minute and follow head position. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Through which two routes can the inflammatory process heal an injury or implant site?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the supplying arterioles, or lymphatic drainage of the exudate","New tissue from native parenchymal cells, or fibroblastic scar tissue","Phagocytosis by neutrophils alone, or opsonization by immunoglobulin G","Hemoconcentration followed by stasis, or leukocyte transmigration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The site heals either by new tissue generated by native parenchymal cells or by formation of fibroblastic scar tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are drusen composed of?","choices":["Calcium and phosphate crystals","Cholesterol esters, as in xanthelasma","Lipofuscin and apolipoproteins","Fibrin and platelet aggregates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — drusen, the hallmark of dry macular degeneration, are discrete yellow extracellular deposits of lipofuscin and apolipoproteins beneath the pigment epithelium. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Adults over fifty worldwide","United States adults aged twenty to seventy-four","United States adults aged over seventy-five years","Children under sixteen worldwide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronic hyperglycemia damages retinal capillaries, and diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in United States adults aged twenty to seventy-four. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of presbyopia?","choices":["The axial globe shortens with age","The cornea flattens progressively with age, reducing its refractive contribution","The retinal pigment epithelium thins with age","The lens stiffens and the ciliary muscle loses accommodation, through sclerosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sclerosis stiffens the lens and the ciliary muscle loses accommodation, so the eye can no longer focus on near objects. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which three processes link chronic hepatitis B infection to hepatocellular carcinoma?","choices":["Chronic inflammation, regenerative hyperplasia, genomic instability","Initiation, promotion, and progression","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, and abnormal differentiation","Self-renewal, immortality, and differentiation capacity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronic infection causes ongoing liver cell injury, stimulating continuous regenerative proliferation of liver cells that are at risk for genetic mutations. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which single event in otosclerosis produces the hearing loss?","choices":["Erosion of the incus by the remodeled bone","Hair cell loss at the cochlear base","Accumulation of endolymph in the scala media","Ankylosis of the stapes footplate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once the footplate is fixed it cannot transfer mechanical vibration into the cochlear fluid, and conduction fails progressively. Fixation is the whole lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
